[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Entity Framework Core (ORM) in C#"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Entity Framework Core (EF Core) is a modern, lightweight, cross-platform, and open-source Object-Relational Mapper (ORM) for .NET. It enables .NET developers to work with a database using .NET objects, eliminating the need to write most of the data-access code themselves. EF Core supports many database engines, including SQL Server, SQLite, PostgreSQL, MySQL, and others."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":".NET SDK (version 6.0 or later)"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"An IDE like Visual Studio or Visual Studio Code"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"A database server (e.g., SQL Server, PostgreSQL)"},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Setting up a Project"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Create a new C# console application or ASP.NET Core web application using the .NET CLI or Visual Studio."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"dotnet new console -o MyEFCoreApp"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Navigate to the project directory:"},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"cd MyEFCoreApp"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Installing EF Core Packages"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Install the necessary NuGet packages for EF Core and the database provider you intend to use. For example, to use SQL Server, you would install 'Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer'."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"dotnet add package Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer
dotnet add package Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Defining the Model"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Create C# classes that represent the tables in your database. These classes are called entities."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"public class Blog
{
    public int BlogId { get; set; }
    public string Title { get; set; }
    public string Content { get; set; }

    public List<Post> Posts { get; set; }
}

public class Post
{
    public int PostId { get; set; }
    public string Title { get; set; }
    public string Content { get; set; }

    public int BlogId { get; set; }
    public Blog Blog { get; set; }
}"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating the DbContext"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Create a class that inherits from 'DbContext'. This class represents a session with the database and allows you to query and save data."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;

public class BloggingContext : DbContext
{
    public BloggingContext(DbContextOptions<BloggingContext> options) : base(options)
    {
    }

    public DbSet<Blog> Blogs { get; set; }
    public DbSet<Post> Posts { get; set; }

    protected override void OnConfiguring(DbContextOptionsBuilder optionsBuilder)
    {
        optionsBuilder.UseSqlServer("Server=(localdb)\mssqllocaldb;Database=MyEFCoreDatabase;Trusted_Connection=True;");
    }
}"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"Note: Replace the connection string in 'OnConfiguring' with your actual database connection string."},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Generating the Database"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"Use EF Core Migrations to create and update the database schema. First, add a migration:"},{"order":24,"type":"code","text":"dotnet ef migrations add InitialCreate"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"Then, apply the migration to create the database:"},{"order":26,"type":"code","text":"dotnet ef database update"},{"order":27,"type":"subheading","text":"Performing CRUD Operations"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"Now you can use the 'DbContext' to perform CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete) operations on your database."},{"order":29,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating Data"},{"order":30,"type":"code","text":"using (var context = new BloggingContext())
{
    var blog = new Blog { Title = "My First Blog", Content = "This is my first blog post." };
    context.Blogs.Add(blog);
    context.SaveChanges();
}"},{"order":31,"type":"subheading","text":"Reading Data"},{"order":32,"type":"code","text":"using (var context = new BloggingContext())
{
    var blogs = context.Blogs.ToList();
    foreach (var blog in blogs)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"Blog Title: {blog.Title}");
    }
}"},{"order":33,"type":"subheading","text":"Updating Data"},{"order":34,"type":"code","text":"using (var context = new BloggingContext())
{
    var blog = context.Blogs.FirstOrDefault(b => b.Title == "My First Blog");
    if (blog != null)
    {
        blog.Content = "Updated content for my first blog.";
        context.SaveChanges();
    }
}"},{"order":35,"type":"subheading","text":"Deleting Data"},{"order":36,"type":"code","text":"using (var context = new BloggingContext())
{
    var blog = context.Blogs.FirstOrDefault(b => b.Title == "My First Blog");
    if (blog != null)
    {
        context.Blogs.Remove(blog);
        context.SaveChanges();
    }
}"},{"order":37,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":38,"type":"paragraph","text":"Entity Framework Core simplifies database interactions in C# applications by providing an ORM layer. This tutorial covered the basic steps to set up EF Core, define models, generate a database, and perform CRUD operations. Further exploration of EF Core features like relationships, querying, and configuration options will enhance your ability to build robust data-driven applications."}]
